From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Robin Snyders <robin@snyders.xyz>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 00:20:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: hotdog: enable the haptics bus

The stock tree puts an Awinic AW8697 at 0x5a on the bus mainline calls i2c7,
and nothing here enabled that bus, so the part could not even be reached.

No upstream driver matches it yet. linux-next carries aw86927, which is the same
family but a later generation: it identifies parts by a two-byte chip id at 0x57
and 0x58, while the vendor sources give this one a single-byte id of 0x97.
Adding AW8697 support to that driver is the work; being able to talk to the chip
is the prerequisite.

Signed-off-by: Robin Snyders <robin@snyders.xyz>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-oneplus-hotdog.dts | 15 +++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-oneplus-hotdog.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-oneplus-hotdog.dts
@@ -539,6 +539,20 @@
  * secure element on this system, and powering it is one of the candidates for
  * the reset seen when the RF field comes up.
  */
+/*
+ * Haptics bus. The stock tree puts an Awinic AW8697 at 0x5a here. No upstream
+ * driver matches it yet: linux-next carries aw86927, which is the same family
+ * but a later generation, with a two-byte chip id where this part reports one.
+ * Enable the bus so the controller can be reached and identified while that
+ * support is written.
+ */
+&i2c7 {
+	/delete-property/ dmas;
+	/delete-property/ dma-names;
+	clock-frequency = <400000>;
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
 &i2c9 {
 	/delete-property/ dmas;
 	/delete-property/ dma-names;
--
2.50.1
